const s=[{id:"optimism-maier-seligman",title:"O01 · Maier & Seligman · 2016 · Learned helplessness at fifty",url:"https://doi.org/10.1037/rev0000033",type:"research",finding:"The review revises the learned-helplessness account: in aversive animal paradigms, prolonged stimulation produces passivity while detecting control engages protective circuitry.",limitation:"Primarily animal experiments, not a pooled human treatment review. It does not establish ordinary human freezing as a universal default or validate a control-list exercise."},{id:"optimism-malouff-schutte",title:"O03 · Malouff & Schutte · 2017 · Can interventions increase optimism?",url:"https://doi.org/10.1080/17439760.2016.1221122",type:"research",finding:"Across 29 randomized studies with 3,319 participants, psychological interventions increased optimism scores on average (g = 0.41).",limitation:"Methods and follow-up varied; shorter follow-up and completer analyses yielded larger effects. The opened record did not verify the confidence interval. This does not establish permanent change or ODA effectiveness."},{id:"optimism-sweeney",title:"O02 · Sweeney, Anderson & Bailey · 1986 · Explanations and depression",url:"https://doi.org/10.1037/0022-3514.50.5.974",type:"research",finding:"Across 104 studies with nearly 15,000 participants, depression was associated with internal, stable, global explanations of negative events.",limitation:"An association does not establish cause or make a negative explanation false. The opened abstract did not verify pooled effects or confidence intervals. A reassuring rewrite is not always accurate."},{id:"optimism-hofmann",title:"O06 · Hofmann et al. · 2012 · CBT review of meta-analyses",url:"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10608-012-9476-1",type:"research",finding:"The review identified 269 CBT meta-analyses and reviewed 106 representative ones. Support concerned treatment packages and varied by problem.",limitation:"Neither count means isolated thought-record trials or validates ODA’s worksheet. A separate erratum’s correction text was unavailable for verification."},{id:"optimism-brunwasser",title:"O05 · Brunwasser, Gillham & Kim · 2009 · Penn Resiliency Program review",url:"https://doi.org/10.1037/a0017671",type:"research",finding:"Across 17 controlled studies with 2,498 young people, the multisession program showed small depressive-symptom benefits against no intervention.",limitation:"Benefits over active controls and prevention of depressive diagnoses were not established. This youth package does not validate an isolated adult ABCDE exercise."},{id:"optimism-ellis-abc",title:"O12 · Albert Ellis Institute · ABC professional explanation",url:"https://albertellis.org/wp-content/uploads/2021/09/Emotional-Disturbance-and-Its-Treatment-in-a-Nutshell-ABCs-.pdf",type:"guidance",finding:"The professional explanation describes an event, beliefs, consequences, disputing rigid beliefs, and a new effective philosophy.",limitation:"A procedure document, not an efficacy trial. Ellis’s final step should not be silently equated with later “energization” variants. A brief ODA exercise is an adaptation."},{id:"optimism-beck-thought-record",title:"O13 · Beck Institute · Thought record worksheet",url:"https://beckinstitute.org/wp-content/uploads/2021/08/Thought-Record-Worksheet.pdf",type:"guidance",finding:"The clinical worksheet documents situation, thought, emotion, evidence, a balanced response, and subsequent ratings.",limitation:"A clinical procedure, not an isolated efficacy trial. ODA’s shorter adaptation is not CBT treatment, and a balanced response need not reduce distress."},{id:"optimism-reivich-mrt",title:"O14 · Reivich, Seligman & McBride · 2011 · Master resilience training",url:"https://doi.org/10.1037/a0021897",type:"guidance",finding:"The program description includes ABC, thinking-trap prompts, and worst, best, and most-likely possibilities followed by a plan.",limitation:"A ten-day trainer-course description. It does not establish standalone efficacy of these exercises or the ODA course."},{id:"optimism-landau-inquiry",title:"O11 · Landau et al. · 2021 · Facilitated inquiry-based stress reduction trial",url:"https://doi.org/10.1001/jamanetworkopen.2021.39670",type:"research",finding:"A registered randomized trial in 100 women with BRCA variants compared a 12-week facilitated inquiry program with standard care and reported benefits on some well-being outcomes.",limitation:"Weekly three-hour groups plus assisted practice are not a brief self-guided lesson. Standard care lacked matched attention. The trial does not establish CBT equivalence or applicability to abuse or trauma."},{id:"optimism-katie-instructions",title:"O15 · Byron Katie International · The Work instructions",url:"https://thework.com/instruction-the-work-byron-katie/",type:"technique",finding:"Practitioner instructions describe four inquiry questions, followed by turnarounds and genuine examples.",limitation:"Instructions are not scientific evidence. Turnarounds must remain optional, with no invented examples or forced self-blame. The longer facilitated trial is a different delivery."},{id:"optimism-nhs-cbt",title:"O16 · NHS · Cognitive behavioural therapy",url:"https://www.nhs.uk/tests-and-treatments/cognitive-behavioural-therapy-cbt/",type:"guidance",finding:"NHS guidance describes structured CBT treatment and routes to professional help.",limitation:"Guidance does not endorse ODA as therapy or guarantee a response. Practical protection and professional care take priority over a self-guided thinking exercise."},{id:"optimism-three-good-things",title:"O07 · Seligman et al. · 2005 · Positive psychology interventions",url:"https://doi.org/10.1037/0003-066X.60.5.410",type:"research",finding:"The original online trial used one-week exercises. Three good things meant three events plus why they happened, nightly for one week.",limitation:"577 baseline respondents became 411 follow-up completers, largely White educated volunteers. Continued practice was not randomized. This does not prove guaranteed maintenance or clinical treatment."},{id:"optimism-mongrain",title:"O08 · Mongrain & Anselmo-Matthews · 2012 · Positive exercise replication",url:"https://doi.org/10.1002/jclp.21839",type:"research",finding:"The replication found happiness gains with named exercises and a positive placebo. A distinctive depression benefit was not demonstrated.",limitation:"The opened abstract did not verify participant count or numerical effects. Results provide less exercise-specific support, without proving every positive exercise useless."},{id:"optimism-nimh-support",title:"O20 · NIMH · Depression guidance",url:"https://www.nimh.nih.gov/health/publications/depression",type:"guidance",finding:"Persistent symptoms or impaired functioning warrant professional evaluation. Crisis support is available.",limitation:"This is guidance, not an app diagnosis. There is no requirement to wait two weeks or complete the course before asking for help."}],e={schemaVersion:2,revision:2,id:"optimism-1",courseId:"optimism",locale:"en",title:"Rejected? Test one part you can influence",goal:"Choose a low-stakes setback and take one useful step within your actual influence.",minutes:{learning:6,suggestedAction:2,review:2},direction:{hook:"One rejection closed the road? Test an available step before deciding what comes next.",reason:{id:"optimism-1-reason-available-step",label:"Which available step have you ruled out, and what evidence supports that?",kind:"text",maxLength:160,optional:!0,suggestions:["Check a ruled-out option","Keep a justified stopping point","Try an available requirement"]},why:{id:"optimism-1-direction",label:"Why does this move matter in your actual week?",kind:"text",maxLength:300,optional:!0}},idea:{paragraphs:["A setback and your next available move are different things. You cannot make someone approve your work. You may be able to ask which requirement was missed, practice a skill, or seek support. Choose one part you can test rather than declaring the whole situation controllable.","Research interventions have changed optimism scores on average. That measures expectations, not proof that a rejection will reverse. This course follows one ordinary setback and one recovery action. Save the step, actually try it, and record the response, including no response. A calendar entry is preparation; asking the question is the act."],example:{title:"Maya's unanswered feedback request",text:"Maya’s draft is rejected, and she considers abandoning a project she cares about. At 12:30, in a safe team message thread, she asks, “Which requirement should I fix first?” She gets no reply that afternoon. Her record says the question was sent. She has tested a feedback request, not proved the project will be accepted."}},move:{invitation:"Choose an ordinary setback. Try one available step, then record what happened.",instructions:["Keep this same setback and direction through the course.","Choose an actual act, not only a calendar entry."],prompts:[],suggestions:{action:["Send one specific feedback question","Correct one known missed requirement","Request practical help with the setback"],minimum:["Send one clear feedback sentence","Fix one known error","Make one practical support request"],cue:["At my next safe team check-in","When I return to the rejected draft","When support is available"],place:["In a safe message thread","At my usual work surface"],fallback:["Ask someone to identify one specific requirement","Work on a known requirement","Switch an unavailable feedback channel"],observation:["Whether I sent the question","The reply or no reply","What was within my influence"],minutes:[2,1,5],when:[{label:"Today",daysFromToday:0},{label:"Tomorrow",daysFromToday:1}]},suggested:{action:"Send one specific feedback question",minimum:"Send one clear feedback sentence",minutes:2,cueHint:"At my next safe team check-in",placeHint:"In a safe message thread",obstacleHint:"I cannot identify a requirement I can change.",fallbackHint:"Ask someone to identify one specific requirement",observationHint:"Whether I sent the question"},stopRule:"Exclude danger, abuse, self-harm, and serious symptoms. Stop repetitive analysis; seek support.",accessibleAlternative:"Use your usual communication method, practical help, or a safe boundary."},result:{question:"Did you do your chosen minimum?",observationPrompt:"What did you actually do, and what happened next?"},judgment:{primary:{id:"optimism-1-review-window-decision",scenario:"Maya’s draft was rejected without a reason. She has ten minutes before the reviewer’s question window closes. A revision needs two hours; she wants to know whether it is worth doing.",question:"Which step best informs that decision now?",options:[{id:"optimism-1-inspect-brief",text:"Inspect the old brief for ten minutes, then revise the requirement she thinks caused the rejection.",feedback:"Checking the brief may reveal a real omission. It still leaves the reviewer’s reason unknown, and uses the closing question window. Ask now; inspect the relevant requirement afterward."},{id:"optimism-1-ask-criterion",text:"Ask which requirement was missed, then use the answer to choose one feasible corrective step.",feedback:"This uses an available information source before it closes. A reply may justify revising, seeking help, or stopping. Sending the question counts as the act; getting a reply does not."},{id:"optimism-1-send-partial-rewrite",text:"Send a ten-minute partial rewrite now to demonstrate initiative before the question window closes.",feedback:"A partial rewrite could help if it addresses the real gap. With no reason supplied, Maya would be guessing. Use this window to ask which change matters before investing in revision."}],bestOptionId:"optimism-1-ask-criterion"}},carry:{takeaway:"After a setback, test one part you can influence and record what it actually changes.",followUp:{questionId:"optimism-1-return",question:"Did you take the available step, and did it produce information, no reply, or a new constraint?",observationPrompt:"What information, no reply, or new constraint followed the step?",adjustmentPrompt:"Which next step fits what you learned?"}},goDeeper:{sections:[{heading:"The animal account was revised",paragraphs:["Maier and Seligman’s 2016 review revisits the learned-helplessness explanation using decades of animal research [O01]. In the reviewed aversive paradigms, prolonged stimulation produces passivity, while detecting control engages protective circuitry. This revised account concerns rats or dogs exposed to prolonged aversive events, not a direct experiment on an adult’s ordinary rejected draft.","It does not establish that freezing is every person’s default, that all helplessness has one mechanism, or that listing controllable tasks reverses a clinical condition. This lesson therefore starts with a practical distinction rather than assigning your reaction a brain explanation: the reviewer’s decision and the question you can send are different events.","ODA’s one-step recovery experiment is an adaptation. You may discover the available lever does little, or that the constraints require help or a different direction. Keep that result. A real attempt provides information without promising restored hope."]},{heading:"Changed expectations are not guaranteed outcomes",paragraphs:["Malouff and Schutte combined 29 randomized studies involving 3,319 participants [O03]. Psychological interventions increased optimism scores on average, with g = 0.41. Methods, delivery, and follow-up varied; shorter follow-up and completer analyses produced larger effects. This average describes study groups, not how much your expectations will change after one attempt.","The review does not show permanent personality change, better objective outcomes, or the effect of this ODA course. A feedback request tests a requirement. It cannot promise future acceptance. The answer can still help you decide whether the revision deserves your time, needs support, or no longer fits.","Your next step may be support rather than another task. NIMH guidance recommends professional evaluation for persistent symptoms or impaired functioning [O20]. You can ask for help before completing lessons and without waiting for a duration threshold. An actual help request can be a meaningful action in its own right."]}],sourceIds:["optimism-maier-seligman","optimism-malouff-schutte","optimism-nimh-support"]}},t={schemaVersion:2,revision:2,id:"optimism-2",courseId:"optimism",locale:"en",title:"One setback becoming your whole story? Get specific",goal:"Keep the true cause of your setback and try one correction without a global self-verdict.",minutes:{learning:6,suggestedAction:2,review:2},direction:{hook:"A rejected draft became “I cannot do this”? Keep the facts and test your next correction.",reason:{id:"optimism-2-reason",label:"What part is yours to change, and what remains uncertain?",kind:"text",maxLength:160,optional:!0,suggestions:["Own the correctable part","Keep uncertainty honest","Resume one useful correction"]},why:{id:"optimism-2-direction",label:"Why does this move matter in your actual week?",kind:"text",maxLength:300,optional:!0}},idea:{paragraphs:["Bring back your setback and recorded response. Explanations that treat setbacks as lasting, affecting many parts of life, and caused by something about you are associated with depression. Association does not prove causation or make every painful explanation wrong.","Ask: how long might the cause last, which part of life does it affect, and what is yours or unknown? A durable skill gap can be real. Name it without making one draft a verdict on everything. Test one correction. Keep responsibility and uncertainty rather than forcing a reassuring cause."],example:{title:"Maya's specific correction",text:"Maya learns that her rejected draft did not answer the client’s budget question. At 7:00, she replaces “I am no good at this” with a specific account: the budget section was incomplete. She adds one usable explanation and sends it for review. The reviewer still wants more detail. Her correction is real, and the overall result stays open."}},move:{invitation:"Use the same setback. Name one accurate cause, then perform the correction it suggests.",instructions:["Keep durable constraints and your own contribution if the evidence supports them.","Do one useful correction; record the actual result."],prompts:[],suggestions:{action:["Correct one known requirement","Ask what missed the brief","Practice the identified skill once"],minimum:["Fix one incomplete draft line","Send one clarifying question","Complete one real skill attempt"],cue:["When I reopen the same draft","At the next safe feedback opening","After reading the actual requirement"],place:["In the draft I am correcting","In a safe feedback channel"],fallback:["Reread the requirement; correct one supported gap","Ask when essential information is missing","Request help with the skill gap"],observation:["What I actually corrected","Which cause remained uncertain","Whether the feedback matched my explanation"],minutes:[2,1,5],when:[{label:"Today",daysFromToday:0},{label:"Tomorrow",daysFromToday:1}]},suggested:{action:"Correct one known requirement",minimum:"Fix one incomplete draft line",minutes:2,cueHint:"When I reopen the same draft",placeHint:"In the draft I am correcting",obstacleHint:"I turn one failure into a verdict about all my work.",fallbackHint:"Reread the requirement; correct one supported gap",observationHint:"What I actually corrected"},stopRule:"Exclude danger, abuse, self-harm, and serious symptoms. Stop repetitive analysis; seek support.",accessibleAlternative:"Use your usual communication method, practical help, or a safe boundary."},result:{question:"Did you do your chosen minimum?",observationPrompt:"What did you actually do, and what happened next?"},judgment:{primary:{id:"optimism-2-budget-gap-decision",scenario:"Maya’s reviewer names one missing budget assumption. The rest meets the brief. She has ten minutes to revise before review closes and thinks the gap proves she cannot do this work.",question:"Which response fits the evidence and deadline?",options:[{id:"optimism-2-rewrite-budget",text:"Rewrite the whole budget section now so the reviewer can judge a fully fresh version.",feedback:"A full rewrite can help when a section has several linked problems. Here one assumption is missing and time is short. Correct that gap without replacing work already accepted."},{id:"optimism-2-clarify-first",text:"Ask the reviewer to restate the missing assumption, then wait for clarification before changing the section.",feedback:"Clarification is useful when feedback is ambiguous. Here the requirement is named, and waiting risks the review window. Make the supported correction, then ask whether it meets the requirement."},{id:"optimism-2-specific-correction",text:"Keep the budget gap as true, correct one element, and leave the wider career conclusion unproven.",feedback:"This owns the supported gap and uses the short window to address it. Ask whether the correction meets the requirement. Further feedback stays evidence about this draft, not a career verdict."}],bestOptionId:"optimism-2-specific-correction"}},carry:{takeaway:"An accurate explanation can be uncomfortable and still point to a useful correction.",followUp:{questionId:"optimism-2-return",question:"Did your correction address a supported cause, and what part of the explanation is still unknown?",observationPrompt:"Which supported cause did you address, and what stayed unknown?",adjustmentPrompt:"Which gap needs correction, clarification, or support next?"}},goDeeper:{sections:[{heading:"Questions, not reassuring answers",paragraphs:["Sweeney and colleagues reviewed 104 studies with nearly 15,000 participants [O02]. Depression was associated with internal, stable, and global explanations for negative events across varied samples and tasks. These dimensions concern personal contribution, lasting causes, and broad effects; they do not require a setback to have only one cause. The association does not show which factor caused which.","This lesson uses the dimensions to inspect an explanation, not to prescribe a cheerful result. A long-term constraint may remain long-term. Your own missed requirement may remain yours to correct. Shared causes may be present, and some causes may still be unknown. Replacing every explanation with “temporary and outside me” would lose accuracy.","Bring the question back to the same course experiment. Which part can you correct, check, or get help with? A specific action is easier to test than a broad claim about ability. Keep the record of the action separate from your interpretation of its outcome."]},{heading:"A fair response still allows a real problem",paragraphs:["The Beck Institute’s thought-record procedure documents evidence, alternatives, a balanced response, and subsequent observations [O13]. It is a clinical worksheet, not proof that this short explanation exercise independently improves mood. ODA’s brief adaptation has no established isolated treatment effect. Use it to identify a supported correction, rather than requiring a reassuring explanation.","A balanced account can include unpleasant facts. The budget section may be incomplete, the deadline may be tight, or the skill may need practice. It can also include what is not known. The next behavior might be correction, a neutral question, or a request for support rather than another reassuring sentence.","NHS guidance describes CBT as structured treatment with professional routes to help [O16]. This course is education. If repeated self-guided analysis increases distress or interferes with functioning, stop the exercise and seek appropriate assistance. Safety and practical needs take priority over an optimism label."]}],sourceIds:["optimism-sweeney","optimism-beck-thought-record","optimism-nhs-cbt"]}},a={schemaVersion:2,revision:2,id:"optimism-3",courseId:"optimism",locale:"en",title:"Angry reply ready? Separate fact from verdict",goal:"Examine your same setback briefly, then send or make one constructive response you can stand behind.",minutes:{learning:6,suggestedAction:2,review:2},direction:{hook:"About to send the defensive reply? Keep the true fact and choose a response worth sending.",reason:{id:"optimism-3-reason",label:"Which response would serve your direction tomorrow as well as today?",kind:"text",maxLength:160,optional:!0,suggestions:["Own a specific mistake","Reply without a career verdict","Solve the practical problem"]},why:{id:"optimism-3-direction",label:"Why does this move matter in your actual week?",kind:"text",maxLength:300,optional:!0}},idea:{paragraphs:["A missed deadline is a fact. “I cannot do this job” is a wider belief. A documented framework separates event, belief, and the feelings or behavior that follow. Thoughts can influence your response, but do not explain every emotion.","Bring back your setback. State one neutral fact, check the belief against evidence, and choose a useful response. If the concern is true, keep it and solve that part. A fair reply can include accountability, a request, or a boundary. Act without requiring embarrassment or anger to disappear."],example:{title:"Maya's accountable reply",text:"Maya misses the revised report deadline. At 6:30, she starts a defensive reply, then separates the fact from “I cannot do this job.” She sends, “I missed today’s deadline. I can deliver the corrected section by noon tomorrow; does that fit?” Her manager accepts the time but remains frustrated. Maya records the actual agreement and her unfinished work."}},move:{invitation:"Use the same setback. Check the belief briefly, then carry out one fair response.",instructions:["Separate one observable fact from the wider verdict.","Send a useful correction, request, or boundary."],prompts:[],suggestions:{action:["Send one accountable corrective reply","Make one realistic deadline request","Complete one agreed correction"],minimum:["Send one constructive sentence","State one feasible next step","Finish one agreed useful part"],cue:["Before sending the difficult reply","At the next safe corrective discussion","When I return to the agreed task"],place:["In the safe work message thread","At my usual work surface"],fallback:["Write one fact and feasible next step; send","Ask someone to check the factual reply","Choose practical support instead"],observation:["What I actually sent or changed","The agreement or lack of agreement","What part of the concern stayed true"],minutes:[2,1,5],when:[{label:"Today",daysFromToday:0},{label:"Tomorrow",daysFromToday:1}]},suggested:{action:"Send one accountable corrective reply",minimum:"Send one constructive sentence",minutes:2,cueHint:"Before sending the difficult reply",placeHint:"In the safe work message thread",obstacleHint:"I want to defend myself before addressing the actual mistake.",fallbackHint:"Write one fact and feasible next step; send",observationHint:"What I actually sent or changed"},stopRule:"Exclude danger, abuse, self-harm, and serious symptoms. Stop repetitive analysis; seek support.",accessibleAlternative:"Use your usual communication method, practical help, or a safe boundary."},result:{question:"Did you do your chosen minimum?",observationPrompt:"What did you actually do, and what happened next?"},judgment:{primary:{id:"optimism-3-decision",scenario:"Maya’s manager correctly says she missed a deadline. Maya thinks, “I cannot do this job,” and wants to defend herself.",question:"What best uses a fair belief check?",options:[{id:"optimism-3-own-and-correct",text:"Name the missed deadline, leave the career verdict unproven, and agree one realistic corrective step.",feedback:"This keeps the supported fact and tests the wider conclusion. A realistic corrective response follows even if embarrassment remains. The manager’s approval is an outcome to observe, not a completion requirement."},{id:"optimism-3-past-reviews",text:"Review her favorable past feedback until the criticism feels less threatening, then decide whether to answer.",feedback:"Past feedback can challenge a global verdict, but it does not address today’s missed deadline. Waiting for relief delays the practical response. Use relevant evidence briefly, then propose a feasible correction."},{id:"optimism-3-pressure-first",text:"Explain the pressure she was under and ask for recognition of her effort before discussing a correction.",feedback:"Workload pressure may be real and may need a boundary or support. Making recognition a condition postpones the correction. Own the specific deadline gap while requesting a realistic adjustment."}],bestOptionId:"optimism-3-own-and-correct"}},carry:{takeaway:"Keep the true fact. Test the wider verdict. Act on the useful conclusion.",followUp:{questionId:"optimism-3-return",question:"Did your actual response address the true problem, even if the difficult feeling remained?",observationPrompt:"Which true problem did your response address?",adjustmentPrompt:"What still needs correction, a request, or a boundary?"}},goDeeper:{sections:[{heading:"Where the letter framework comes from",paragraphs:["The Albert Ellis Institute’s professional explanation describes the event, beliefs, and consequences, followed by disputing rigid beliefs and developing a new effective philosophy [O12]. Later resilience training variants use “energization” for a final step [O14]. Those labels are not interchangeable descriptions of a guaranteed emotional change.","These sources document procedures. They do not establish the isolated efficacy of a brief adult ABCDE exercise. ODA uses a shorter sequence: describe the fact, inspect the belief, and carry out a proportionate response. Feelings also reflect conditions, loss, health, and support; a belief map is not a complete explanation of emotion.","For your continuing experiment, use the map only when it resolves an observed problem. You do not need five letters every time you act. If the concern is valid, the useful response may be practical problem solving or support rather than disputing it away."]},{heading:"A youth package is not this single exercise",paragraphs:["Brunwasser and colleagues reviewed 17 controlled Penn Resiliency Program studies involving 2,498 young people [O05]. Against no intervention, depressive-symptom effects were small: d = 0.11 immediately, 0.21 at six to eight months, and 0.20 at twelve months. Benefits over active controls and prevention of depressive diagnoses were not established.","The program taught multiple cognitive and behavioral skills. Those findings do not validate a single adult ABCDE round or predict the effect of ODA’s response exercise. Learning the framework, saving a plan, and sending a constructive reply remain separate events. Only the reported reply is evidence of that real action.","Keep the course experiment bounded to an ordinary, safe setback. A factual missed deadline can call for correction without becoming a statement about worth. Where a plan exceeds your time or resources, revise the demand or ask for help rather than repeating the same self-disputation."]}],sourceIds:["optimism-ellis-abc","optimism-reivich-mrt","optimism-brunwasser"]}},i={schemaVersion:2,revision:2,id:"optimism-4",courseId:"optimism",locale:"en",title:"No reply yet? Check what your worry knows",goal:"Distinguish a known fact from a prediction, then check one available fact about your setback.",minutes:{learning:6,suggestedAction:2,review:2},direction:{hook:"No answer, and your mind has supplied a verdict? Check one fact before planning around it.",reason:{id:"optimism-4-reason",label:"What evidence would change the next step you are taking?",kind:"text",maxLength:160,optional:!0,suggestions:["Check before guessing","Keep a real concern visible","Ask for the missing fact"]},why:{id:"optimism-4-direction",label:"Why does this move matter in your actual week?",kind:"text",maxLength:300,optional:!0}},idea:{paragraphs:["Bring back your setback. “No reply today” is known. “They have rejected me again” is a prediction unless evidence supports it. A balanced response keeps true concerns and missing information together. It can stay uncertain or unpleasant.","A documented thought record is a clinical tool. Evidence for full treatment does not prove a short worksheet works alone. Use a bounded check: known fact, guessed meaning, available evidence, next act. Ask a neutral question or correct the supported problem. Stop if repeated analysis increases distress. A lower worry rating is not required."],example:{title:"Maya's missing reply",text:"Maya has no reply to her revised section and knows her manager is traveling. At their scheduled check-in, she asks whether the budget explanation meets the brief. The answer is “Almost; add the assumption.” She makes that correction. She had feared a broader rejection, but her record keeps the actual feedback rather than declaring all her worries unfounded."}},move:{invitation:"Check one available fact about the same setback. Act on what it supports.",instructions:["Separate what is known from what is guessed.","Ask one neutral question or correct the supported problem."],prompts:[],suggestions:{action:["Ask one neutral status question","Check the actual requirement once","Correct the confirmed problem"],minimum:["Send one fact-checking sentence","Verify one relevant requirement","Fix one supported error"],cue:["At our scheduled safe check-in","When the relevant evidence is available","Before acting on the prediction"],place:["In the safe review channel","Beside the actual requirement"],fallback:["Choose a later suitable fact check","Request practical help instead","Stop repetitive analysis and pause"],observation:["What was known before the check","What the actual answer established","Whether my plan changed with evidence"],minutes:[2,1,5],when:[{label:"Today",daysFromToday:0},{label:"Tomorrow",daysFromToday:1}]},suggested:{action:"Ask one neutral status question",minimum:"Send one fact-checking sentence",minutes:2,cueHint:"At our scheduled safe check-in",placeHint:"In the safe review channel",obstacleHint:"I keep analyzing an uncertainty that needs actual information.",fallbackHint:"Choose a later suitable fact check",observationHint:"What was known before the check"},stopRule:"Exclude danger, abuse, self-harm, and serious symptoms. Stop repetitive analysis; seek support.",accessibleAlternative:"Use your usual communication method, practical help, or a safe boundary."},result:{question:"Did you do your chosen minimum?",observationPrompt:"What did you actually do, and what happened next?"},judgment:{primary:{id:"optimism-4-deadline-check-decision",scenario:"Maya has no reply to her draft. Her traveling manager can discuss it at today’s check-in. Submission closes in thirty minutes; either a correction or a backup proposal will take twenty.",question:"Which first move best uses the remaining time?",options:[{id:"optimism-4-submit-current",text:"Submit the current draft now because travel could explain the silence and the deadline is close.",feedback:"Submitting protects the deadline, but travel does not establish that the draft meets the brief. A brief check-in can identify a correction while leaving twenty minutes to make it."},{id:"optimism-4-check-uncertainty",text:"Keep the cause uncertain, ask about the draft at the check-in, and revise from the actual answer.",feedback:"This checks the relevant uncertainty while the manager is available and time remains to act. The answer may support correction or a backup. Reduced worry is not the test."},{id:"optimism-4-backup-and-timeline",text:"Start the backup proposal and message for a decision timeline while keeping both routes open.",feedback:"A backup preserves options when feedback is unavailable. Here the manager is available now, and either route takes twenty minutes. Check the draft first so the remaining time serves the supported need."}],bestOptionId:"optimism-4-check-uncertainty"}},carry:{takeaway:"A balanced response keeps the true worry, the uncertainty, and a useful way to check.",followUp:{questionId:"optimism-4-return",question:"What did the checked fact establish, and did your next plan change with that evidence?",observationPrompt:"What did the evidence establish, including what remains unknown?",adjustmentPrompt:"Which next step should change with that evidence?"}},goDeeper:{sections:[{heading:"Treatment evidence belongs to the package",paragraphs:["Hofmann and colleagues identified 269 CBT meta-analyses and reviewed 106 representative ones [O06]. These are review counts, not 269 isolated tests of thought records. The paper summarizes treatment packages with different components and problems; it has no single pooled participant count or universal effect for this exercise.","Treatment packages combine several methods and support. Their evidence cannot identify the effect of this brief fact check by itself. Use the check to answer a practical uncertainty: what requirement matters, whether a correction is needed, or when a decision will arrive. If no new information is available, keep the uncertainty visible instead of repeating the analysis.","The Beck Institute worksheet documents a clinical procedure [O13]. It does not independently establish its isolated effect. This lesson uses an abbreviated adaptation to make one prediction inspectable, then connects that inspection to the same practical action you are already working on."]},{heading:"Ratings are observations, not targets",paragraphs:["The documented thought-record procedure includes thought and emotion ratings, evidence, a balanced response, and subsequent ratings [O13]. Those numbers can describe what changed. A required drop would instead pressure you to record the preferred answer. The balanced response should fit the evidence even when concern remains credible.","ODA does not require a thinking-trap label or a particular emotional score. “Known” and “guessed” may be enough to choose one fact check. A supported concern can call for correction, a boundary, or practical support. Another reassuring rewrite is not always the next useful move.","NHS guidance describes CBT as structured professional treatment [O16]. Self-guided analysis that increases distress or repeats without new information can be stopped. Seek appropriate help when needed. Keep the actual answer and behavior in your course record, rather than using reduced worry as proof that the real task was done."]}],sourceIds:["optimism-hofmann","optimism-beck-thought-record","optimism-nhs-cbt"]}},n={schemaVersion:2,revision:2,id:"optimism-5",courseId:"optimism",locale:"en",title:"Canceled again? Stay curious and make a request",goal:"Examine a low-stakes assumption about your same setback, then make a clear safe request or boundary.",minutes:{learning:6,suggestedAction:2,review:2},direction:{hook:"Canceled reviews feel personal? Stay curious without dropping the request you still need.",reason:{id:"optimism-5-reason",label:"What request or boundary remains necessary after reflection?",kind:"text",maxLength:160,optional:!0,suggestions:["Ask directly instead of guessing","Protect a needed boundary","Stay curious without taking all the blame"]},why:{id:"optimism-5-direction",label:"Why does this move matter in your actual week?",kind:"text",maxLength:300,optional:!0}},idea:{paragraphs:["Their motives can be uncertain while your need remains real. Bring back your setback. When a safe review is canceled, “They do not care” may go beyond the facts. “I need a review time I can use” can remain true.","A longer facilitated inquiry program has randomized evidence; that does not prove four self-guided questions work as treatment. Use inquiry only if useful, then make the request or boundary. You do not have to reverse a thought into self-blame or invent examples. Exclude danger and mistreatment. Curiosity does not require contact or surrendering protection."],example:{title:"Maya's clear review request",text:"Maya’s colleague cancels their report review twice. At 8:00, she questions her assumption that he does not care, without deciding the opposite is true. She sends, “Can you review by Thursday? If not, I will use another reviewer.” He cannot commit. Maya records her request and arranges another safe review. The disappointment remains."}},move:{invitation:"For this safe uncertainty, make the request or boundary your experiment needs.",instructions:["Keep genuine needs and limits visible.","Send one clear request; agreement is not your minimum."],prompts:[],suggestions:{action:["Ask for a workable review time","State one practical review boundary","Use another safe feedback route"],minimum:["Send one clear timing request","State one limit respectfully","Request one alternative review"],cue:["After a safe review cancellation","At our next suitable check-in","When the agreed limit is reached"],place:["In the safe review message thread","Through my usual communication channel"],fallback:["Choose another safe reviewer","Use practical support instead","Pause inquiry that increases distress"],observation:["The actual request I made","The reply including no agreement","Whether the boundary still fit"],minutes:[2,1,5],when:[{label:"Today",daysFromToday:0},{label:"Tomorrow",daysFromToday:1}]},suggested:{action:"Ask for a workable review time",minimum:"Send one clear timing request",minutes:2,cueHint:"After a safe review cancellation",placeHint:"In the safe review message thread",obstacleHint:"Reflection is replacing a direct request I still need.",fallbackHint:"Choose another safe reviewer",observationHint:"The actual request I made"},stopRule:"Exclude danger, abuse, self-harm, and serious symptoms. No forced self-blame; pause distressing inquiry.",accessibleAlternative:"Use your usual communication method, practical help, or a safe boundary."},result:{question:"Did you do your chosen minimum?",observationPrompt:"What did you actually do, and what happened next?"},judgment:{primary:{id:"optimism-5-decision",scenario:"A safe colleague has canceled Maya’s review twice. His reasons are unclear, and she needs feedback this week.",question:"Which response preserves curiosity and a useful limit?",options:[{id:"optimism-5-finish-alone",text:"Finish the review alone to reduce tension, then reconsider asking the colleague on a later project.",feedback:"Working independently can fit when review is optional or you have the skill. Here Maya still needs feedback this week. Ask for a workable time or use another safe reviewer."},{id:"optimism-5-flexible-slot",text:"Offer another flexible review slot and keep the arrangement open so the colleague can choose when available.",feedback:"Flexibility can preserve cooperation, but two cancellations and this week’s deadline make an open arrangement risky. Pair a workable time with an alternative so the real feedback need remains covered."},{id:"optimism-5-request-with-limit",text:"Keep his motives open, ask for a workable review time, and name an alternative if unavailable.",feedback:"This combines uncertainty with a clear practical need. The colleague may decline, and Maya can then use the alternative. Sending the request is her action; cooperation is an outcome she does not control."}],bestOptionId:"optimism-5-request-with-limit"}},carry:{takeaway:"Question the uncertain story while keeping the real request or boundary intact.",followUp:{questionId:"optimism-5-return",question:"What did your request or boundary change, and does the same limit still serve your direction?",observationPrompt:"What changed after your request or boundary, including no reply?",adjustmentPrompt:"Does the limit still fit, or should it change?"}},goDeeper:{sections:[{heading:"The randomized trial was intensive and facilitated",paragraphs:["Landau and colleagues tested inquiry-based stress reduction in a registered randomized trial of 100 women with BRCA variants [O11]. The intervention lasted twelve weeks, with weekly three-hour groups and one hour of assisted practice, compared with standard care. It reported benefits on some well-being outcomes, but the control did not match attention.","These findings do not establish that four brief self-guided questions are treatment, that the method equals CBT, or that results apply to abuse or trauma. The population, dose, and delivery differ substantially from this ordinary report-review example. A request needs a practical reason, not a promise of clinical improvement.","ODA’s request exercise is a teaching adaptation. If reflection helps, bring it back to the actual review you need. Ask for a usable time and keep an alternative available. You can finish the request while its effect stays unknown; another person’s cooperation is not a test of your optimism."]},{heading:"Optional inquiry, without forced reversals",paragraphs:["Byron Katie’s practitioner instructions use four questions: whether a thought is true, whether you can be certain, how holding it affects your response, and what changes when you loosen it [O15]. They then describe turnarounds with genuine examples. This is a procedure source, not evidence that the isolated questions are effective treatment.","Any reversal stays optional. You do not need a fixed count of examples, and you should not invent them. A reversal that shifts responsibility for harm onto you is not a required discovery. A clear request or a boundary can remain necessary after reflection, even when another person’s motives are uncertain.","Keep inquiry to safe, ordinary frustrations. NHS guidance provides professional routes for treatment [O16]; ODA does not replace them. Danger, abuse, self-harm concerns, and serious symptoms call for protection and appropriate support. You can stop this exercise and continue with a practical response."]}],sourceIds:["optimism-landau-inquiry","optimism-katie-instructions","optimism-nhs-cbt"]}},o={schemaVersion:2,revision:2,id:"optimism-6",courseId:"optimism",locale:"en",title:"Rough week? Keep the useful step going",goal:"Consolidate your same recovery experiment using actual attempts, helpful conditions, and one feasible next opportunity.",minutes:{learning:6,suggestedAction:2,review:2},direction:{hook:"The week stayed hard. Which useful action or support is worth repeating?",reason:{id:"optimism-6-reason",label:"What helped your direction, and what problem still needs action?",kind:"text",maxLength:160,optional:!0,suggestions:["Repeat a helpful condition","Keep the unresolved problem visible","Choose one feasible next attempt"]},why:{id:"optimism-6-direction",label:"Why does this move matter in your actual week?",kind:"text",maxLength:300,optional:!0}},idea:{paragraphs:["Review your same setback and action. What did you do, what helped, and what remains unresolved? A hard week can contain useful support. Choose the next opportunity from those facts without demanding a positive feeling.","The original “three good things” study used three events and their causes nightly for one week. A replication found happiness gains with a positive placebo too, weakening claims that the named exercise uniquely works. You may notice one genuine helpful event here, or none. Keep your recovery experiment without a new nightly ritual."],example:{title:"Maya's useful cause to repeat",text:"On Friday, Maya’s report still needs approval. Her record shows one useful cause: asking another reviewer produced a specific correction. At her desk, she applies that correction and books the next suitable review. She notes the help without calling the week a success. Her next experiment keeps the same report, a smaller task, and an honest return date."}},move:{invitation:"Review your same experiment. Repeat one helpful condition and choose the next feasible recovery action.",instructions:["Keep unresolved problems visible alongside genuine help.","Choose one next attempt, support step, or pause."],prompts:[],suggestions:{action:["Apply the correction my feedback revealed","Repeat my useful recovery step","Ask for the support that helped"],minimum:["Fix one supported draft element","Complete one useful recovery unit","Send one specific support request"],cue:["At my next suitable draft session","When useful feedback is available","At my chosen support opening"],place:["In my existing draft","In the safe support channel"],fallback:["Shrink the actual recovery step","Pause and choose a return date","Use another feasible support route"],observation:["What helped the actual action","What problem remained unresolved","Whether the next step fit my week"],minutes:[2,1,5],when:[{label:"Today",daysFromToday:0},{label:"Tomorrow",daysFromToday:1}]},suggested:{action:"Apply the correction my feedback revealed",minimum:"Fix one supported draft element",minutes:2,cueHint:"At my next suitable draft session",placeHint:"In my existing draft",obstacleHint:"I am adding rituals instead of improving the useful action.",fallbackHint:"Shrink the actual recovery step",observationHint:"What helped the actual action"},stopRule:"Exclude danger, abuse, self-harm, and serious symptoms. Stop repetitive analysis; seek support.",accessibleAlternative:"Use your usual communication method, practical help, or a safe boundary."},result:{question:"Did you do your chosen minimum?",observationPrompt:"What did you actually do, and what happened next?"},judgment:{primary:{id:"optimism-6-correction-scope-decision",scenario:"Maya’s reviewer identified one missing assumption and confirmed the rest of her report meets the brief. She has twenty minutes for the five-minute correction before submission closes; approval remains uncertain.",question:"Which next act best uses the helpful feedback?",options:[{id:"optimism-6-repeat-helpful-cause",text:"Apply the confirmed correction, submit within the window, and record the result without predicting approval.",feedback:"This uses the specific feedback while keeping approval uncertain. The correction fits the available time and serves the same report. Record what was changed and any response, including none."},{id:"optimism-6-check-scope",text:"Request another scope check from the reviewer before revising, so she avoids overlooking a wider problem.",feedback:"Another check can help when the scope is unclear. Here the reviewer already confirmed it. Waiting could consume the submission window without resolving a new uncertainty; use the specific correction first."},{id:"optimism-6-request-extension",text:"Request a short extension so she can review the entire report before applying the correction.",feedback:"An extension can protect work when time or capacity is insufficient. Here the identified correction fits. Requesting more time adds coordination without addressing a stated obstacle; broader review can wait."}],bestOptionId:"optimism-6-repeat-helpful-cause"}},carry:{takeaway:"Carry forward a helpful cause and a feasible action. Let the week’s difficulty remain true.",followUp:{questionId:"optimism-6-return",question:"Did repeating the helpful condition support your next action, and what still needs correction, support, or stopping?",observationPrompt:"What condition helped, and what remains unresolved?",adjustmentPrompt:"Repeat that condition, change the act, get support, or stop?"}},goDeeper:{sections:[{heading:"Three events plus causes was the studied procedure",paragraphs:["Seligman and colleagues’ original online trial asked participants to record three things that went well and why each happened, nightly for one week [O07]. It began with 577 respondents, but the analysis used 411 follow-up completers, largely White, educated online volunteers. The exercises and an early-memory comparison were randomized; continued practice was not.","The original trial reported changes on happiness and depression questionnaires. It does not establish that continued practice caused later maintenance, that ten minutes is a required dose, or that gratitude treats depression. A later replication found happiness improvements with a positive placebo as well and did not demonstrate a distinctive depression benefit [O08].","You can choose the original week-long exercise separately if it fits. Noticing one good or neutral event, or pausing on a difficult night, is a humane adaptation rather than the tested procedure. This course ending instead consolidates your existing recovery action."]},{heading:"A useful next week can remain uncertain",paragraphs:["The resilience-training program description documents considering worst, best, and most-likely possibilities before making a plan [O14]. It does not establish standalone efficacy for that short exercise. Use it only if a specific uncertainty in your existing experiment needs planning, rather than adding it as another required ritual.","Realistic attention keeps both the useful condition and the remaining problem. A reviewer’s help can matter while the report remains unapproved. The helpful cause may be asking a specific question, making time available, or using support. Repeat it only when it fits the next opportunity; no feeling of gratitude is required.","Review the same factual records before next week. Keep a meaningful minimum, a recognizable cue, and a fallback you can use. Choose support, rest, a boundary, or stopping when appropriate. Future plans, reflection, and quiz answers remain separate from actual attempts. Bring back what happened, including uncertainty or no reply."]}],sourceIds:["optimism-three-good-things","optimism-mongrain","optimism-reivich-mrt"]}},r={id:"optimism",title:"One setback taking over? Find your next useful step",subtitle:"Six lessons to examine one setback, take a proportionate recovery step, and learn from the actual result.",description:"Bring one ordinary setback back into proportion. Keep the painful facts, check uncertain interpretations, and choose a practical response that serves the life you want.",outcome:"One setback examined fairly, a recovery action you can try, and a return plan grounded in what actually happened.",scope:"For adults who tend to give up, blame themselves or expect the worst after setbacks. The course draws on Martin Seligman’s learned optimism, cognitive behavioral therapy (CBT) and Byron Katie’s The Work. It is self-help, not a treatment for depression, anxiety or any other condition, and it does not replace therapy or medication. If hopelessness persists or ordinary days become hard to get through, talk to a doctor or mental health professional. You can seek help sooner. For thoughts of suicide or an emotional crisis in the US, call or text 988; elsewhere use local crisis support. For immediate danger, use local emergency services.",photo:{id:"1465188162913-8fb5709d6d57",alt:"A woman walking along a hilltop path in golden evening light"},lessons:[{id:"optimism-1",schemaVersion:2,v2:e,title:"Rejected? Test one part you can influence",minutes:6,goal:"Choose a low-stakes setback and take one useful step within your actual influence.",reading:["A setback and your next available move are different things. You cannot make someone approve your work. You may be able to ask which requirement was missed, practice a skill, or seek support. Choose one part you can test rather than declaring the whole situation controllable.","Research interventions have changed optimism scores on average. That measures expectations, not proof that a rejection will reverse. This course follows one ordinary setback and one recovery action. Save the step, actually try it, and record the response, including no response. A calendar entry is preparation; asking the question is the act."],practice:["Choose an ordinary setback. Try one available step, then record what happened.","Try the saved minimum at your chosen cue, or record a pause.","Record the actual result here; use the dated return to choose your next attempt."],reflection:"Which available step have you ruled out, and what evidence supports that?",question:"Maya’s draft was rejected without a reason. She has ten minutes before the reviewer’s question window closes. A revision needs two hours; she wants to know whether it is worth doing. Which step best informs that decision now?",options:["Inspect the old brief for ten minutes, then revise the requirement she thinks caused the rejection.","Ask which requirement was missed, then use the answer to choose one feasible corrective step.","Send a ten-minute partial rewrite now to demonstrate initiative before the question window closes."],correct:1,feedback:"This uses an available information source before it closes. A reply may justify revising, seeking help, or stopping. Sending the question counts as the act; getting a reply does not.",takeaway:"After a setback, test one part you can influence and record what it actually changes.",sources:["optimism-maier-seligman","optimism-malouff-schutte","optimism-nimh-support"],deeper:[{heading:"The animal account was revised",paragraphs:["Maier and Seligman’s 2016 review revisits the learned-helplessness explanation using decades of animal research [O01]. In the reviewed aversive paradigms, prolonged stimulation produces passivity, while detecting control engages protective circuitry. This revised account concerns rats or dogs exposed to prolonged aversive events, not a direct experiment on an adult’s ordinary rejected draft.","It does not establish that freezing is every person’s default, that all helplessness has one mechanism, or that listing controllable tasks reverses a clinical condition. This lesson therefore starts with a practical distinction rather than assigning your reaction a brain explanation: the reviewer’s decision and the question you can send are different events.","ODA’s one-step recovery experiment is an adaptation. You may discover the available lever does little, or that the constraints require help or a different direction. Keep that result. A real attempt provides information without promising restored hope."]},{heading:"Changed expectations are not guaranteed outcomes",paragraphs:["Malouff and Schutte combined 29 randomized studies involving 3,319 participants [O03]. Psychological interventions increased optimism scores on average, with g = 0.41. Methods, delivery, and follow-up varied; shorter follow-up and completer analyses produced larger effects. This average describes study groups, not how much your expectations will change after one attempt.","The review does not show permanent personality change, better objective outcomes, or the effect of this ODA course. A feedback request tests a requirement. It cannot promise future acceptance. The answer can still help you decide whether the revision deserves your time, needs support, or no longer fits.","Your next step may be support rather than another task. NIMH guidance recommends professional evaluation for persistent symptoms or impaired functioning [O20]. You can ask for help before completing lessons and without waiting for a duration threshold. An actual help request can be a meaningful action in its own right."]}],example:{title:"Maya's unanswered feedback request",text:"Maya’s draft is rejected, and she considers abandoning a project she cares about. At 12:30, in a safe team message thread, she asks, “Which requirement should I fix first?” She gets no reply that afternoon. Her record says the question was sent. She has tested a feedback request, not proved the project will be accepted."},photo:{id:"1527580094867-0e719f119120",alt:"A hiker crossing grey rocks in the early morning, waiting for sunrise"}},{id:"optimism-2",schemaVersion:2,v2:t,title:"One setback becoming your whole story? Get specific",minutes:6,goal:"Keep the true cause of your setback and try one correction without a global self-verdict.",reading:["Bring back your setback and recorded response. Explanations that treat setbacks as lasting, affecting many parts of life, and caused by something about you are associated with depression. Association does not prove causation or make every painful explanation wrong.","Ask: how long might the cause last, which part of life does it affect, and what is yours or unknown? A durable skill gap can be real. Name it without making one draft a verdict on everything. Test one correction. Keep responsibility and uncertainty rather than forcing a reassuring cause."],practice:["Use the same setback. Name one accurate cause, then perform the correction it suggests.","Try the saved minimum at your chosen cue, or record a pause.","Record the actual result here; use the dated return to choose your next attempt."],reflection:"What part is yours to change, and what remains uncertain?",question:"Maya’s reviewer names one missing budget assumption. The rest meets the brief. She has ten minutes to revise before review closes and thinks the gap proves she cannot do this work. Which response fits the evidence and deadline?",options:["Rewrite the whole budget section now so the reviewer can judge a fully fresh version.","Ask the reviewer to restate the missing assumption, then wait for clarification before changing the section.","Keep the budget gap as true, correct one element, and leave the wider career conclusion unproven."],correct:2,feedback:"This owns the supported gap and uses the short window to address it. Ask whether the correction meets the requirement. Further feedback stays evidence about this draft, not a career verdict.",takeaway:"An accurate explanation can be uncomfortable and still point to a useful correction.",sources:["optimism-sweeney","optimism-beck-thought-record","optimism-nhs-cbt"],deeper:[{heading:"Questions, not reassuring answers",paragraphs:["Sweeney and colleagues reviewed 104 studies with nearly 15,000 participants [O02]. Depression was associated with internal, stable, and global explanations for negative events across varied samples and tasks. These dimensions concern personal contribution, lasting causes, and broad effects; they do not require a setback to have only one cause. The association does not show which factor caused which.","This lesson uses the dimensions to inspect an explanation, not to prescribe a cheerful result. A long-term constraint may remain long-term. Your own missed requirement may remain yours to correct. Shared causes may be present, and some causes may still be unknown. Replacing every explanation with “temporary and outside me” would lose accuracy.","Bring the question back to the same course experiment. Which part can you correct, check, or get help with? A specific action is easier to test than a broad claim about ability. Keep the record of the action separate from your interpretation of its outcome."]},{heading:"A fair response still allows a real problem",paragraphs:["The Beck Institute’s thought-record procedure documents evidence, alternatives, a balanced response, and subsequent observations [O13]. It is a clinical worksheet, not proof that this short explanation exercise independently improves mood. ODA’s brief adaptation has no established isolated treatment effect. Use it to identify a supported correction, rather than requiring a reassuring explanation.","A balanced account can include unpleasant facts. The budget section may be incomplete, the deadline may be tight, or the skill may need practice. It can also include what is not known. The next behavior might be correction, a neutral question, or a request for support rather than another reassuring sentence.","NHS guidance describes CBT as structured treatment with professional routes to help [O16]. This course is education. If repeated self-guided analysis increases distress or interferes with functioning, stop the exercise and seek appropriate assistance. Safety and practical needs take priority over an optimism label."]}],example:{title:"Maya's specific correction",text:"Maya learns that her rejected draft did not answer the client’s budget question. At 7:00, she replaces “I am no good at this” with a specific account: the budget section was incomplete. She adds one usable explanation and sends it for review. The reviewer still wants more detail. Her correction is real, and the overall result stays open."},photo:{id:"1509029032154-54ba8b3216d4",alt:"A man in glasses looking thoughtfully out of a window"}},{id:"optimism-3",schemaVersion:2,v2:a,title:"Angry reply ready? Separate fact from verdict",minutes:6,goal:"Examine your same setback briefly, then send or make one constructive response you can stand behind.",reading:["A missed deadline is a fact. “I cannot do this job” is a wider belief. A documented framework separates event, belief, and the feelings or behavior that follow. Thoughts can influence your response, but do not explain every emotion.","Bring back your setback. State one neutral fact, check the belief against evidence, and choose a useful response. If the concern is true, keep it and solve that part. A fair reply can include accountability, a request, or a boundary. Act without requiring embarrassment or anger to disappear."],practice:["Use the same setback. Check the belief briefly, then carry out one fair response.","Try the saved minimum at your chosen cue, or record a pause.","Record the actual result here; use the dated return to choose your next attempt."],reflection:"Which response would serve your direction tomorrow as well as today?",question:"Maya’s manager correctly says she missed a deadline. Maya thinks, “I cannot do this job,” and wants to defend herself. What best uses a fair belief check?",options:["Name the missed deadline, leave the career verdict unproven, and agree one realistic corrective step.","Review her favorable past feedback until the criticism feels less threatening, then decide whether to answer.","Explain the pressure she was under and ask for recognition of her effort before discussing a correction."],correct:0,feedback:"This keeps the supported fact and tests the wider conclusion. A realistic corrective response follows even if embarrassment remains. The manager’s approval is an outcome to observe, not a completion requirement.",takeaway:"Keep the true fact. Test the wider verdict. Act on the useful conclusion.",sources:["optimism-ellis-abc","optimism-reivich-mrt","optimism-brunwasser"],deeper:[{heading:"Where the letter framework comes from",paragraphs:["The Albert Ellis Institute’s professional explanation describes the event, beliefs, and consequences, followed by disputing rigid beliefs and developing a new effective philosophy [O12]. Later resilience training variants use “energization” for a final step [O14]. Those labels are not interchangeable descriptions of a guaranteed emotional change.","These sources document procedures. They do not establish the isolated efficacy of a brief adult ABCDE exercise. ODA uses a shorter sequence: describe the fact, inspect the belief, and carry out a proportionate response. Feelings also reflect conditions, loss, health, and support; a belief map is not a complete explanation of emotion.","For your continuing experiment, use the map only when it resolves an observed problem. You do not need five letters every time you act. If the concern is valid, the useful response may be practical problem solving or support rather than disputing it away."]},{heading:"A youth package is not this single exercise",paragraphs:["Brunwasser and colleagues reviewed 17 controlled Penn Resiliency Program studies involving 2,498 young people [O05]. Against no intervention, depressive-symptom effects were small: d = 0.11 immediately, 0.21 at six to eight months, and 0.20 at twelve months. Benefits over active controls and prevention of depressive diagnoses were not established.","The program taught multiple cognitive and behavioral skills. Those findings do not validate a single adult ABCDE round or predict the effect of ODA’s response exercise. Learning the framework, saving a plan, and sending a constructive reply remain separate events. Only the reported reply is evidence of that real action.","Keep the course experiment bounded to an ordinary, safe setback. A factual missed deadline can call for correction without becoming a statement about worth. Where a plan exceeds your time or resources, revise the demand or ask for help rather than repeating the same self-disputation."]}],example:{title:"Maya's accountable reply",text:"Maya misses the revised report deadline. At 6:30, she starts a defensive reply, then separates the fact from “I cannot do this job.” She sends, “I missed today’s deadline. I can deliver the corrected section by noon tomorrow; does that fit?” Her manager accepts the time but remains frustrated. Maya records the actual agreement and her unfinished work."},photo:{id:"1643148636630-0b0fb138fc74",alt:"A person writing in a notebook with a pen"}},{id:"optimism-4",schemaVersion:2,v2:i,title:"No reply yet? Check what your worry knows",minutes:6,goal:"Distinguish a known fact from a prediction, then check one available fact about your setback.",reading:["Bring back your setback. “No reply today” is known. “They have rejected me again” is a prediction unless evidence supports it. A balanced response keeps true concerns and missing information together. It can stay uncertain or unpleasant.","A documented thought record is a clinical tool. Evidence for full treatment does not prove a short worksheet works alone. Use a bounded check: known fact, guessed meaning, available evidence, next act. Ask a neutral question or correct the supported problem. Stop if repeated analysis increases distress. A lower worry rating is not required."],practice:["Check one available fact about the same setback. Act on what it supports.","Try the saved minimum at your chosen cue, or record a pause.","Record the actual result here; use the dated return to choose your next attempt."],reflection:"What evidence would change the next step you are taking?",question:"Maya has no reply to her draft. Her traveling manager can discuss it at today’s check-in. Submission closes in thirty minutes; either a correction or a backup proposal will take twenty. Which first move best uses the remaining time?",options:["Submit the current draft now because travel could explain the silence and the deadline is close.","Keep the cause uncertain, ask about the draft at the check-in, and revise from the actual answer.","Start the backup proposal and message for a decision timeline while keeping both routes open."],correct:1,feedback:"This checks the relevant uncertainty while the manager is available and time remains to act. The answer may support correction or a backup. Reduced worry is not the test.",takeaway:"A balanced response keeps the true worry, the uncertainty, and a useful way to check.",sources:["optimism-hofmann","optimism-beck-thought-record","optimism-nhs-cbt"],deeper:[{heading:"Treatment evidence belongs to the package",paragraphs:["Hofmann and colleagues identified 269 CBT meta-analyses and reviewed 106 representative ones [O06]. These are review counts, not 269 isolated tests of thought records. The paper summarizes treatment packages with different components and problems; it has no single pooled participant count or universal effect for this exercise.","Treatment packages combine several methods and support. Their evidence cannot identify the effect of this brief fact check by itself. Use the check to answer a practical uncertainty: what requirement matters, whether a correction is needed, or when a decision will arrive. If no new information is available, keep the uncertainty visible instead of repeating the analysis.","The Beck Institute worksheet documents a clinical procedure [O13]. It does not independently establish its isolated effect. This lesson uses an abbreviated adaptation to make one prediction inspectable, then connects that inspection to the same practical action you are already working on."]},{heading:"Ratings are observations, not targets",paragraphs:["The documented thought-record procedure includes thought and emotion ratings, evidence, a balanced response, and subsequent ratings [O13]. Those numbers can describe what changed. A required drop would instead pressure you to record the preferred answer. The balanced response should fit the evidence even when concern remains credible.","ODA does not require a thinking-trap label or a particular emotional score. “Known” and “guessed” may be enough to choose one fact check. A supported concern can call for correction, a boundary, or practical support. Another reassuring rewrite is not always the next useful move.","NHS guidance describes CBT as structured professional treatment [O16]. Self-guided analysis that increases distress or repeats without new information can be stopped. Seek appropriate help when needed. Keep the actual answer and behavior in your course record, rather than using reduced worry as proof that the real task was done."]}],example:{title:"Maya's missing reply",text:"Maya has no reply to her revised section and knows her manager is traveling. At their scheduled check-in, she asks whether the budget explanation meets the brief. The answer is “Almost; add the assumption.” She makes that correction. She had feared a broader rejection, but her record keeps the actual feedback rather than declaring all her worries unfounded."},photo:{id:"1681396059172-7532b1ef8b47",alt:"An open journal lying on a table in soft morning light"}},{id:"optimism-5",schemaVersion:2,v2:n,title:"Canceled again? Stay curious and make a request",minutes:6,goal:"Examine a low-stakes assumption about your same setback, then make a clear safe request or boundary.",reading:["Their motives can be uncertain while your need remains real. Bring back your setback. When a safe review is canceled, “They do not care” may go beyond the facts. “I need a review time I can use” can remain true.","A longer facilitated inquiry program has randomized evidence; that does not prove four self-guided questions work as treatment. Use inquiry only if useful, then make the request or boundary. You do not have to reverse a thought into self-blame or invent examples. Exclude danger and mistreatment. Curiosity does not require contact or surrendering protection."],practice:["For this safe uncertainty, make the request or boundary your experiment needs.","Try the saved minimum at your chosen cue, or record a pause.","Record the actual result here; use the dated return to choose your next attempt."],reflection:"What request or boundary remains necessary after reflection?",question:"A safe colleague has canceled Maya’s review twice. His reasons are unclear, and she needs feedback this week. Which response preserves curiosity and a useful limit?",options:["Finish the review alone to reduce tension, then reconsider asking the colleague on a later project.","Offer another flexible review slot and keep the arrangement open so the colleague can choose when available.","Keep his motives open, ask for a workable review time, and name an alternative if unavailable."],correct:2,feedback:"This combines uncertainty with a clear practical need. The colleague may decline, and Maya can then use the alternative. Sending the request is her action; cooperation is an outcome she does not control.",takeaway:"Question the uncertain story while keeping the real request or boundary intact.",sources:["optimism-landau-inquiry","optimism-katie-instructions","optimism-nhs-cbt"],deeper:[{heading:"The randomized trial was intensive and facilitated",paragraphs:["Landau and colleagues tested inquiry-based stress reduction in a registered randomized trial of 100 women with BRCA variants [O11]. The intervention lasted twelve weeks, with weekly three-hour groups and one hour of assisted practice, compared with standard care. It reported benefits on some well-being outcomes, but the control did not match attention.","These findings do not establish that four brief self-guided questions are treatment, that the method equals CBT, or that results apply to abuse or trauma. The population, dose, and delivery differ substantially from this ordinary report-review example. A request needs a practical reason, not a promise of clinical improvement.","ODA’s request exercise is a teaching adaptation. If reflection helps, bring it back to the actual review you need. Ask for a usable time and keep an alternative available. You can finish the request while its effect stays unknown; another person’s cooperation is not a test of your optimism."]},{heading:"Optional inquiry, without forced reversals",paragraphs:["Byron Katie’s practitioner instructions use four questions: whether a thought is true, whether you can be certain, how holding it affects your response, and what changes when you loosen it [O15]. They then describe turnarounds with genuine examples. This is a procedure source, not evidence that the isolated questions are effective treatment.","Any reversal stays optional. You do not need a fixed count of examples, and you should not invent them. A reversal that shifts responsibility for harm onto you is not a required discovery. A clear request or a boundary can remain necessary after reflection, even when another person’s motives are uncertain.","Keep inquiry to safe, ordinary frustrations. NHS guidance provides professional routes for treatment [O16]; ODA does not replace them. Danger, abuse, self-harm concerns, and serious symptoms call for protection and appropriate support. You can stop this exercise and continue with a practical response."]}],example:{title:"Maya's clear review request",text:"Maya’s colleague cancels their report review twice. At 8:00, she questions her assumption that he does not care, without deciding the opposite is true. She sends, “Can you review by Thursday? If not, I will use another reviewer.” He cannot commit. Maya records her request and arranges another safe review. The disappointment remains."},photo:{id:"1503541517233-120571491cf3",alt:"A man sitting on a park bench with his eyes closed, surrounded by trees"}},{id:"optimism-6",schemaVersion:2,v2:o,title:"Rough week? Keep the useful step going",minutes:6,goal:"Consolidate your same recovery experiment using actual attempts, helpful conditions, and one feasible next opportunity.",reading:["Review your same setback and action. What did you do, what helped, and what remains unresolved? A hard week can contain useful support. Choose the next opportunity from those facts without demanding a positive feeling.","The original “three good things” study used three events and their causes nightly for one week. A replication found happiness gains with a positive placebo too, weakening claims that the named exercise uniquely works. You may notice one genuine helpful event here, or none. Keep your recovery experiment without a new nightly ritual."],practice:["Review your same experiment. Repeat one helpful condition and choose the next feasible recovery action.","Try the saved minimum at your chosen cue, or record a pause.","Record the actual result here; use the dated return to choose your next attempt."],reflection:"What helped your direction, and what problem still needs action?",question:"Maya’s reviewer identified one missing assumption and confirmed the rest of her report meets the brief. She has twenty minutes for the five-minute correction before submission closes; approval remains uncertain. Which next act best uses the helpful feedback?",options:["Apply the confirmed correction, submit within the window, and record the result without predicting approval.","Request another scope check from the reviewer before revising, so she avoids overlooking a wider problem.","Request a short extension so she can review the entire report before applying the correction."],correct:0,feedback:"This uses the specific feedback while keeping approval uncertain. The correction fits the available time and serves the same report. Record what was changed and any response, including none.",takeaway:"Carry forward a helpful cause and a feasible action. Let the week’s difficulty remain true.",sources:["optimism-three-good-things","optimism-mongrain","optimism-reivich-mrt"],deeper:[{heading:"Three events plus causes was the studied procedure",paragraphs:["Seligman and colleagues’ original online trial asked participants to record three things that went well and why each happened, nightly for one week [O07]. It began with 577 respondents, but the analysis used 411 follow-up completers, largely White, educated online volunteers. The exercises and an early-memory comparison were randomized; continued practice was not.","The original trial reported changes on happiness and depression questionnaires. It does not establish that continued practice caused later maintenance, that ten minutes is a required dose, or that gratitude treats depression. A later replication found happiness improvements with a positive placebo as well and did not demonstrate a distinctive depression benefit [O08].","You can choose the original week-long exercise separately if it fits. Noticing one good or neutral event, or pausing on a difficult night, is a humane adaptation rather than the tested procedure. This course ending instead consolidates your existing recovery action."]},{heading:"A useful next week can remain uncertain",paragraphs:["The resilience-training program description documents considering worst, best, and most-likely possibilities before making a plan [O14]. It does not establish standalone efficacy for that short exercise. Use it only if a specific uncertainty in your existing experiment needs planning, rather than adding it as another required ritual.","Realistic attention keeps both the useful condition and the remaining problem. A reviewer’s help can matter while the report remains unapproved. The helpful cause may be asking a specific question, making time available, or using support. Repeat it only when it fits the next opportunity; no feeling of gratitude is required.","Review the same factual records before next week. Keep a meaningful minimum, a recognizable cue, and a fallback you can use. Choose support, rest, a boundary, or stopping when appropriate. Future plans, reflection, and quiz answers remain separate from actual attempts. Bring back what happened, including uncertainty or no reply."]}],example:{title:"Maya's useful cause to repeat",text:"On Friday, Maya’s report still needs approval. Her record shows one useful cause: asking another reviewer produced a specific correction. At her desk, she applies that correction and books the next suitable review. She notes the help without calling the week a success. Her next experiment keeps the same report, a smaller task, and an honest return date."},photo:{id:"1750885847789-f5748b585e3a",alt:"A woman on a balcony looking out over a city"}}]};export{r as COURSE,e as OPTIMISM_1_V2,t as OPTIMISM_2_V2,a as OPTIMISM_3_V2,i as OPTIMISM_4_V2,n as OPTIMISM_5_V2,o as OPTIMISM_6_V2,s as SOURCES};
